# Project: FoundryVTT + Local LLM Co-DM ("dungeon-maister")

You are working on a FoundryVTT **client module** that turns a local LLM into a
narrative co-DM for solo D&D 5e play. Read this file fully before doing any task.

## What we are building
A Foundry V14 client module that runs inside the GM's browser session, reads game
state directly, sends a compact snapshot to a local LLM (LM Studio, OpenAI-compatible
API at http://localhost:1234/v1), and posts the generated narrative back to Foundry
chat. Mechanics (dice, HP, conditions) are resolved by Foundry's existing `dnd5e`
system — NOT by us.

## Locked architectural decisions (do NOT deviate)
1. **In-Foundry client module.** All code runs client-side inside a logged-in GM
   session. Read state via the global `game.*` API (`game.scenes.current`,
   `game.actors`, `game.messages`, `game.journal`, `canvas.tokens`). There is NO
   external Foundry REST API and NO Foundry "API key" — do not invent one.
2. **Reuse the `dnd5e` system for ALL mechanics.** Never hand-roll a rules engine.
   Use the dnd5e system's own methods (e.g. `Actor#rollAttack`, `rollSavingThrow`,
   `rollSkill`, active effects) for any dice, damage, HP, or condition changes.
3. **LLM does narrative only.** It never rolls dice or applies rules. When it needs a
   mechanical action it emits a **structured JSON intent**; our code validates it and
   routes it to the dnd5e system. Mechanical actions are GM-confirmed, never blind writes.
4. **LLM output is structured** via tool-calling / JSON-schema, never regex-parsed prose.
5. **Config** lives in `game.settings.register` (LM Studio URL, model id, temperature),
   NOT in `module.json`.

## Hard constraints
- Target Foundry **V14** (`compatibility` range in the manifest).
- ES modules (`esmodules` in the manifest), modern JS. No build step / no bundler
  unless a task explicitly says so — keep it plain JS that Foundry loads directly.
- The module depends on the `dnd5e` system (declare it in the manifest `relationships`).
- CORS/mixed-content is a known risk: the browser calls `http://localhost:1234`. Assume
  LM Studio has CORS enabled; do not work around it in code without a task saying so.

## Critical working rules
- **Verify APIs before using them. Do NOT trust method or field names from memory or
  from these hints.** Foundry and dnd5e APIs change between versions. Before calling any
  `game.*`, `Actor#*`, `module.json` field, or dnd5e method, confirm it exists in the
  installed version — check the official V14 API docs (https://foundryvtt.com/api/), the
  installed dnd5e system source, or an existing installed module. If you cannot verify a
  signature, leave a `// TODO: verify <thing>` comment rather than guessing.
- Keep each change scoped to exactly what the task card asks. Do not refactor unrelated
  code or add features not in the card.
- Match existing file layout and naming. Put module source under `src/` unless the card
  says otherwise; the manifest stays at repo root or `dist/` per the card.
- After a task, state plainly what you did, which files changed, and anything you could
  NOT verify (so it can be escalated).

## Where things live
- `planning/foundry-llm-dm.md` — the full project plan. Read for background, not per-task.
- `planning/tasks/` — self-contained task cards. Work ONE card at a time. `README.md`
  there explains the card format and the recommended order.

## Definition of done for any card
Code runs without console errors when the module loads in Foundry; the card's explicit
acceptance criteria are met; unverifiable APIs are flagged with TODOs; no unrelated
changes.
